            <ArticleTitle>Subacute thyroiditis causing thyrotoxic crisis; a case report with literature review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>112-114</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2210-2612(17)30113-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijscr.2017.02.041</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Subacute thyroiditis is a self-limited, inflammatory viral thyroid disease which presents with neck pain, usually accompanied by systemic symptoms. On the other hand, thyroid storm is a clinical condition of severe sudden hyperthyroidism accompanied by physiologic de-compensation. We presented a 29-year-old male with features of subacute thyroiditis and thyroid storm who is the third reported case managed by steroid, beta-blocker and analgesics.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">subacute thyroiditis may present with thyrotoxic crisis which respond dramatically to corticosteroid therapy.</AbstractText>
